    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, and it passes. The implementation is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country E.164 national-number length validation.\n\n**New file \u2014 `E164NationalNumberRule.java`**: a small, single-responsibility class holding the known country-code \u2192 national-digit-count rules (`+61` \u2192 9, `+1` \u2192 10) and a `hasValidNationalLength(String e164)` check. It strips the `+`, matches country codes longest-first (so a longer code wins over a shorter prefix), and returns `true` for unknown country codes (leaving them constrained only by the general E.164 shape).\n\n**`TelephoneValidator.java`**: now normalizes to E.164 and additionally enforces the country-length rule, so a wrong national length is rejected \u2014 producing a 400 via the existing Bean Validation flow.\n\n**`Telephone.java`**: updated the javadoc to document the new rule.\n\nI kept the responsibilities separate: `TelephoneNormalizer` still does normalization (formatting strip, default country, general 8\u201315 shape), while the new class owns the per-country length policy. The validator composes both.\n\nVerification:\n- `errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` (`+61 123`, wrong for `+61`) \u2192 400 \u2713\n- The valid AU number produced by `uniqueTelephone()` (`04XXXXXXXX` \u2192 `+61` + 9 national digits) still passes \u2713\n- Seed data is inserted via SQL and never re-validated, so it's unaffected.\n- Full `./mvnw test` \u2192 BUILD SUCCESS.",
